Partie 1 Contenu:
The genesis of the Common is inseparable from the collapse of the **Second Empire** during the **<Franco-Prussian War> of 1870**. After the disaster of **Sedan** and the capture of **<Napoleon III>**, the Republic was proclaimed on September 4, but the conflict bogged down. Paris suffered a merciless four-month siege by **Bismarck's** troops. Deprived of supplies, Parisians endured extreme famine, reduced to consuming horses, then animals from the Jardin des Plantes, and finally rats. Despite this resilience, the national defense government signed a **capitulation** deemed infamous by the Parisian people.

In February 1871, legislative elections brought a monarchist and pacifist majority to power, led by **<Adolphe Thiers>**. The gap widened between an assembly retreated to **Versailles**, perceived as treacherous and reactionary, and a republican Paris, armed by the **National Guard**. Tension culminated on **March 18, 1871**, when Thiers ordered the army to seize the 227 cannons of the National Guard stored on the **Montmartre** hill. The population intervened; instead of opening fire, the soldiers fraternized with the crowd. Generals Lecomte and Thomas were executed by the insurgents. Panicked, Thiers evacuated all administrative services and the army to Versailles, leaving the field open to the **Central Committee** of the National Guard.

{ During the siege of Paris, luxury restaurants offered surreal menus for Christmas 1870, including elephant consommé, kangaroo stew, or even antelope fillet with truffles, all coming from the zoo's menagerie.}

Partie 4 Contenu:
Despite its brevity, the Paris Commune established itself as a pillar of global political mythology. For the **<Workers' International>**, and particularly for **Karl Marx**, it constituted the first historical illustration of the "dictatorship of the proletariat", a moment when the worker seized their own destiny without the mediation of the bourgeoisie.

Repression had the paradoxical effect of cementing the unity of the European labor movement while installing the **Third Republic** on a conservative basis. Adolphe Thiers, by crushing the "rabble", proved to the province that the Republic could be synonymous with order. However, many reforms of the Common (secularism, social rights) would be progressively integrated into republican law a few decades later. Today, the Common remains a major reference for social movements, symbolizing the aspiration for a radical and egalitarian democracy.

{ It was during the Common that the red flag officially became the emblem of Paris, replacing the tricolor flag deemed too linked to monarchist and bourgeois betrayals.}

Partie 3 Contenu:
The end of the Belle Époque was as sudden as it was tragic. The system of diplomatic alliances, which was theoretically supposed to guarantee peace through the balance of power, would instead precipitate the continent's fall. The assassination of Archduke **Franz Ferdinand** in Sarajevo on **June 28, 1914**, served as the detonator for a powder keg of nationalist rivalries accumulated over decades.

In five weeks, the gearing of mobilizations made war inevitable. On **August 4, 1914**, when the United Kingdom declared war on Germany, the dream of an interconnected and peaceful Europe evaporated. The writer **Stefan Zweig**, in his memoirs titled **"The World of Yesterday"**, paints a portrait of this irreversible shift: the collapse of a civilization that believed itself eternal.

Soldiers left for the front convinced that the conflict would be short, a mere formality before Christmas. Yet they were swept away in a total, industrial, and murderous war that would last four years. The Belle Époque died in the mud of the trenches, giving way to a 20th century marked by crises and totalitarianisms.

{In 1913, the creation of the ballet "The Rite of Spring" by Igor Stravinsky in Paris caused such a scandal that spectators came to blows, illustrating the radical modernity that was then shaking up traditional artistic codes.}

{At the start of the war in 1914, to urgently transport reinforcements to the Marne front, 600 Parisian taxis were requisitioned (the famous "taxis of the Marne"), symbolizing the brutal transition from urban carelessness to war logistics.}

|| The Belle Époque (1871-1914) is a Period of extreme contrasts, combining dazzling technical progress for the bourgeoisie and working-class misery, before brutally sinking into the chaos of the Great War. ||

Partie 2 Contenu:
On the evening of **December 1, 1955**, the bus driven by James Blake was full. The driver ordered four Black passengers in the central zone to stand up to make room for a single white passenger. Only Rosa Parks refused to move. She was not physically exhausted, but politically determined. Her arrest for violation of city codes triggered a lightning mobilization.

{Another woman, Claudette Colvin, had refused to give up her seat nine months before Rosa Parks at the age of 15, but The NAACP had not supported her because she was pregnant and single, judging her image too unstable for public opinion.}

{Rosa Parks had already had an altercation with the same driver, James Blake, in 1943. He had evicted her from the bus because she refused to get off to enter through the back door after paying her fare at the front.}

Starting the next day, community leaders and women's organizations distributed thousands of flyers calling for people to no longer use the buses on the day of the trial, **December 5**. To coordinate the action over the long term, they founded the **Montgomery Improvement Association** (MIA). At its head, they placed a newcomer, Pastor **Martin Luther King Jr.**, whose eloquence and doctrine of Christian non-violence would galvanize the troops.

[Rosa Parks arrest Montgomery bus]

Success was immediate: on December 5, the buses ran empty. What was only supposed to last one day turned into an economic and political standoff that would last **381 days**.

Partie 1 Contenu:
Since 1947, the United States and the USSR had been engaged in a global ideological and strategic struggle. The balance tipped when **Fidel Castro** took power in Cuba in **1959**, establishing a revolutionary regime less than 150 kilometers from the Florida coast. For Washington, the presence of a Moscow ally in its "backyard" was unacceptable. Under the **John F. Kennedy** administration, the CIA orchestrated the **<Bay of Pigs>** landing in **April 1961**. The operation was a total disaster: the Cuban exiles were crushed by Castro's army, humiliating Kennedy and pushing Cuba to officially throw itself into the arms of the Soviet Union.

Soviet leader **Nikita Khrushchev** saw this alliance as a major strategic opportunity. The United States had already deployed **<Jupiter>** missiles in **Italy** and **Turkey**, capable of reaching Moscow in minutes. Khrushchev decided to balance the threat by secretly installing medium-range missiles on Cuban soil. This operation, named <Anadyr>, aimed to place Washington under the direct fire of the atom, while protecting the Cuban ally from a new American invasion.

{The transport of missiles to Cuba was camouflaged by sending tons of Russian agricultural equipment and winter clothes to deceive intelligence services about their actual destination.}

The trial of Socrates takes place in a climate of collective paranoia. Athens is emerging from the traumatic **Peloponnesian War** and has recently overthrown the bloody dictatorship of the **Thirty Tyrants**. Although the main accuser, **Meletus**, invokes official motives of **impiety** (not believing in the gods of the city) and **corruption of the youth**, the real reasons are deeply political. Socrates is perceived as a threat because he was the teacher of controversial figures like **Critias**, the leader of the tyrants, or **Alcibiades**, the deserting general.

In reality, Socrates is disruptive because of his method of systematic questioning of certainties. The city seeks a scapegoat for its military and moral failures. The accusation of impiety is particularly serious in a society where religion and Politics are inseparable: doubting the gods amounts to shaking the foundations of the State.

{In 2012, a mock trial of Socrates was organized in Athens with renowned international lawyers. Unlike the original verdict of Antiquity, the modern jury acquitted the philosopher by a narrow majority.}

Although the story of the lovers of Verona now belongs to the collective imagination, it is not a pure invention of **William Shakespeare**. The author drew his main source from the Italian storyteller **Matteo Bandello**, whose 1554 novella had already been translated into English in narrative poems. However, Shakespeare's genius lies in his capacity for condensation. Where the original sources spread the action over several months, Shakespeare tightens the plot over a lightning duration of **five days**. This temporal acceleration transforms a simple thwarted romance into an infernal machine where every minute counts, making the catastrophe inevitable.

At approximately 31 years old at the time of writing, Shakespeare established an innovative dramatic structure by alternating styles. The play begins like a comedy, with bawdy puns and lively street scenes, only to shift brutally into tragedy during the mortal duel between the families. This break in tone is embodied by the Figure of **Mercutio**, whose spirit of <Cynicism> and premature death mark the end of lightheartedness. By sacrificing the brightest and funniest Figure of the play as early as the third act, Shakespeare signals to the viewer that the time for laughter is over and that destiny now demands its due.

{In the original version by Matteo Bandello, Juliet wakes up just before Romeo expires, allowing the two lovers to exchange a final goodbye. Shakespeare removed this moment to make the ending crueler and to highlight the tragic irony of bad timing.}

Partie 3 Contenu:
Paradoxically, Shakespeare showed no interest in the systematic publication of his Work during his lifetime. For him, the dramatic text was a living material, a score intended for the <Lord Chamberlain's Men> troupe rather than an Object for reading. Upon his death, half of his plays existed only in the form of scattered working manuscripts. It was not until 1623, seven years after his passing, that two of his fellow actors undertook to compile his works into the famous <First Folio>.

This compilation is one of the most precious books in History, as it saved from oblivion 18 plays that would never have been published otherwise, including "Macbeth," "Julius Caesar," or "The Tempest." Aesthetically, Shakespearean theater is distinguished by a bold mix of genres that long shocked purists. By having scenes of high political tragedy coexist with the buffoonery of servants or gravediggers, Shakespeare offers a total and baroque vision of existence. This dialectics structure prefigures contemporary cinema, alternating dramatic tension and comic breathing.

{Shakespeare left no autograph manuscript of his plays; his only known writing comes from six signatures on legal documents, including his will where he bequeaths to his wife his 'second best bed'.}

Partie 1 Contenu:
Before being the terrifying monster that the name **Gorgon** instantly evokes, **Medusa** was, according to Ovid's <Metamorphoses>, a young woman of dazzling beauty, particularly proud of her hair. She served as a priestess in the temple of **Athena**, goddess of wisdom and war.

Her beauty unfortunately attracted the attention of **Poseidon**, the god of the seas, who took her by force within the very sacred sanctuary. In a tragic logic specific to antiquity, the profanation of the temple was blamed on the victim rather than the aggressor. Furious, **Athena** transformed Medusa into a hybrid creature: her silky hair became a mass of hissing **snakes** and her eyes took the power to instantly **petrify** anyone who crossed her gaze.

[Medusa Gorgon snakes hair Caravaggio painting]

Excluded from the society of men and gods, she was banished to the edges of the known <World> with her two sisters, Stheno and Euryale, becoming the symbol of absolute dread. She now resides in a cave surrounded by the statues of those who, through curiosity or misfortune, one day laid eyes on her.

{The blood from the left side of Medusa was considered a deadly poison, while the blood from her right side could resurrect the dead, a secret that the goddess Athena later entrusted to Asclepius.}

{According to the poet Lucan, as Perseus flew over Libya with the severed head, drops of blood fell to the ground, giving birth to all the venomous snakes that now inhabit the Libyan desert.}

Aztec thought is based on a cyclical vision of time, illustrated by the myth of the <Five Suns>. According to this tradition, four eras (suns) followed one another, each ending in a global catastrophe. We currently live in the **fifth sun**, named <Nahui Ollin> (4-Movement), an era that, according to prophecy, will die through earthquakes.

[Aztec Five Suns Teotihuacan pyramid sun stone]

This World could only come into being thanks to a foundational act of divine self-sacrifice that occurred at **Teotihuacán**. The gods gathered there to decide who would light the universe. Two deities threw themselves into a ritual brazier to become the sun and the moon, but the sun remained motionless in the sky. To give it the energy necessary for its daily course, all the other gods had to offer their own blood.

This Event establishes the fundamental notion of **blood debt**: since the gods died so that men might live, men must, in return, feed the gods so that the cycle does not stop. Sacrifice is therefore not an act of hatred, but a ritual of reciprocity. Blood is perceived as a sacred vital energy, the <chalchihuatl> (precious water), essential to the metabolism of the universe.

{The Great Temple of Tenochtitlán was not just a place of worship, it was considered the exact center of the universe, the junction point between the underworld, the earth, and the heavens.}

{The Aztecs took the threat of earthquakes so seriously that they had a 'warning' system: they placed bowls of water on the ground to observe ripples before feeling the tremors.}
